 "drivingQuestion": "Why does the mishna rank stoning above burning?",
 "outcome": "resolved",
 "outcomeNote": "the gemara grounds the first step of the mishna's order",
 "continuesQuestionOf": null,
 "segments": [
  {
   "id": "sanhedrin.49b.17",
   "daf": "49b",
   "sentenceId": 16,
   "he": "סְקִילָה שְׂרֵיפָה כּוּ׳. סְקִילָה חֲמוּרָה מִשְּׂרֵיפָה, שֶׁכֵּן נִיתְּנָה לִמְגַדֵּף וּלְעוֹבֵד עֲבוֹדָה זָרָה. מַאי חוּמְרָא? שֶׁכֵּן פּוֹשֵׁט יָדוֹ בָּעִיקָּר.",
   "en": "§ The mishna states the types of the death penalty in descending order of severity: «Stoning, burning,» killing by decapitation, and strangulation. The Gemara discusses the basis for this order. «Stoning» is considered more «severe than burning, as» stoning «is meted out to one who blasphemes,» i.e., one who curses God, «and to one who worships idols.» For «what» reason is the «severity» of these transgressions considered greater than others? «Because» the transgressor «undermines the fundamental» tenets of Judaism."
  }
 ],
